 "label": "Clothes and shoes",
 "topic": "Clothes & wearing",
 "rationale": "The everyday on/off pairs for what you're dressed in: clothes go on with kiru, shoes with haku, and both come off with nugu.",
 "mixUps": "kiru vs haku: kiru for the torso or whole body, haku for anything you step into; nugu takes both off, so drill kiru/haku against the one nugu (fuku kite / kutsu haite / kutsu nuide).",
 "targets": [
  {
   "id": "c-fuku-o-kiru",
   "romaji": "fuku o kiru",
   "polite": "fuku o kimasu",
   "english": "put on / wear clothes",
   "ja": "服を着る",
   "kana": "ふくをきる",
   "politeJa": "服を着ます",
   "noun": "fuku",
   "particle": "o",
   "verb": "kiru",
   "verbJa": "着る",
   "verbSense": "put on or wear on the body (tops, coats, dresses)",
   "englishVerb": "wear",
   "trap": "kiru only covers tops and whole-body clothes (shirt, coat, dress, kimono). English 'wear' also covers shoes, trousers, hats and glasses, which take other verbs (haku, kaburu, kakeru).",
   "partners": [
    "shatsu (shirt)",
    "kooto (coat)",
    "seetaa (sweater)",
    "kimono (kimono)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Mostly heard as kiteru (is wearing, a state, not 'putting on now') or kiteku (wear when going out); casual speech drops o. kiteru 'wearing' is flat (ki-TE-RU) while kiteru 'has come' falls after ki (KI-te-ru), so pitch as well as context tells them apart.",
   "notes": "Off: fuku o nugu. Changing into other clothes is fuku o kigaeru; looking good in them is fuku ga niau (niau ne).",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "ashita donna fuku kiteku?",
    "ja": "明日どんな服着てく？",
    "english": "What are you wearing tomorrow?"
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "ashita wa donna fuku o kite ikimasu ka?",
    "ja": "明日はどんな服を着ていきますか？",
    "english": "What are you wearing tomorrow?"
   }
  },
  {
